\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces Model-free segmentation on one view (camera \texttt  {static2}, frame 75). From left, the input frame, the empty-room plate, their per-pixel change magnitude $d_{c,t}$, extracted silhouette(mask), segmented image and a ground truth (with a light error).}}{49}{figure.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bgsub-montage}{{3.16}{49}{Model-free segmentation on one view (camera \texttt {static2}, frame 75). From left, the input frame, the empty-room plate, their per-pixel change magnitude $d_{c,t}$, extracted silhouette(mask), segmented image and a ground truth (with a light error)}{figure.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Segmentation accuracy against the ground-truth silhouettes. (a)~Threshold sweep. precision falls at low $\tau _s$ (shadow and indirect-light leak), recall falls at high $\tau _s$ (low-contrast subject regions). (b)~Erosion sweep at the chosen threshold, where each final $3\times 3$ erosion step raises precision monotonically, the direct cure for an oversized mask, at a growing cost in recall. Two steps are adopted as the default. (c)~Per-frame IoU of each camera at the chosen setting.}}{50}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bgsub-quality}{{3.17}{50}{Segmentation accuracy against the ground-truth silhouettes. (a)~Threshold sweep. precision falls at low $\tau _s$ (shadow and indirect-light leak), recall falls at high $\tau _s$ (low-contrast subject regions). (b)~Erosion sweep at the chosen threshold, where each final $3\times 3$ erosion step raises precision monotonically, the direct cure for an oversized mask, at a growing cost in recall. Two steps are adopted as the default. (c)~Per-frame IoU of each camera at the chosen setting}{figure.caption.36}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.3.2.1}Shadow-Aware Hysteresis}{51}{subsubsection.3.3.2.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-hysteresis}{{3.3.2.1}{51}{Shadow-Aware Hysteresis}{subsubsection.3.3.2.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.18}{\ignorespaces The camouflage failure and its sweep-guided repair, at reconstruction frame $0$ (camera \texttt  {static1}). With the original setting ($\tau _s = 0.1$, no erosion) the subject's colours match the rug, the silhouette develops holes, and the reconstruction inherits a fragmented lower body. The tuned setting ($\tau _s = 0.06$, two erosions) recovers most of the silhouette and the reconstructed subject is correspondingly more complete.}}{51}{figure.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bgsub-fix}{{3.18}{51}{The camouflage failure and its sweep-guided repair, at reconstruction frame $0$ (camera \texttt {static1}). With the original setting ($\tau _s = 0.1$, no erosion) the subject's colours match the rug, the silhouette develops holes, and the reconstruction inherits a fragmented lower body. The tuned setting ($\tau _s = 0.06$, two erosions) recovers most of the silhouette and the reconstructed subject is correspondingly more complete}{figure.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces Segmentation variants against the ground-truth silhouettes. (a)~Mask quality of the production single threshold, the max-channel difference, and the hysteresis mechanism with its two ablations. Without the shadow veto the weak band floods through the contact shadow (precision $0.37$). Without connectivity the veto alone leaves isolated bounce-light islands. (b)~Per-frame IoU, where the single threshold dips wherever the subject camouflages (the shaded window is the rug), while the shadow-aware hysteresis stays at ${\sim }0.96$ throughout.}}{52}{figure.caption.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seg-variants}{{3.19}{52}{Segmentation variants against the ground-truth silhouettes. (a)~Mask quality of the production single threshold, the max-channel difference, and the hysteresis mechanism with its two ablations. Without the shadow veto the weak band floods through the contact shadow (precision $0.37$). Without connectivity the veto alone leaves isolated bounce-light islands. (b)~Per-frame IoU, where the single threshold dips wherever the subject camouflages (the shaded window is the rug), while the shadow-aware hysteresis stays at ${\sim }0.96$ throughout}{figure.caption.38}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.3}Visual-Hull Initialization}{52}{subsection.3.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-hull}{{3.3.3}{52}{Visual-Hull Initialization}{subsection.3.3.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces The camouflage frame (camera \texttt  {static1}, frame 3) under both segmenters. The amplified change magnitude (second panel) shows the weak but nonzero evidence over the rug-camouflaged lower body. The single threshold fragments it, the shadow-aware hysteresis recovers a silhouette visually indistinguishable from the ground truth.}}{53}{figure.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seg-montage}{{3.20}{53}{The camouflage frame (camera \texttt {static1}, frame 3) under both segmenters. The amplified change magnitude (second panel) shows the weak but nonzero evidence over the rug-camouflaged lower body. The single threshold fragments it, the shadow-aware hysteresis recovers a silhouette visually indistinguishable from the ground truth}{figure.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.21}{\ignorespaces Frame-0 reconstruction from three initializations under identical losses. (a)~The hull seed converges fastest. An equal-budget random cloud collapses, its off-object primitives faded by the silhouette term and pruned. A $30\times $ larger random budget converges more slowly to a slightly higher final value. The periodic dips are \textsc  {splatfacto}'s scheduled opacity resets during densification. (b)~Surviving primitive counts (log scale). The $50$k random start pays a ${\sim }3.5\times $ larger cloud for its $+3.4$\,dB.}}{53}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hull-init}{{3.21}{53}{Frame-0 reconstruction from three initializations under identical losses. (a)~The hull seed converges fastest. An equal-budget random cloud collapses, its off-object primitives faded by the silhouette term and pruned. A $30\times $ larger random budget converges more slowly to a slightly higher final value. The periodic dips are \textsc {splatfacto}'s scheduled opacity resets during densification. (b)~Surviving primitive counts (log scale). The $50$k random start pays a ${\sim }3.5\times $ larger cloud for its $+3.4$\,dB}{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.22}{\ignorespaces Effect of the velocity warm start, measured per frame on a 40-frame tracking sequence from an identical frame-0 state (improved pipeline, with hysteresis silhouettes and margin clamping active in both arms). (a)~Steps needed to reach a fixed $20$\,dB object-PSNR target, with means of $23$ vs.\ $145$ steps ($84\%$ fewer). (b)~Quality reached at a PSNR plateau, where the warm-started fit converges about $5.9$\,dB higher, because a cold start must re-traverse the full inter-frame motion each frame and tends to stall early.}}{54}{figure.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:velocity-conv}{{3.22}{54}{Effect of the velocity warm start, measured per frame on a 40-frame tracking sequence from an identical frame-0 state (improved pipeline, with hysteresis silhouettes and margin clamping active in both arms). (a)~Steps needed to reach a fixed $20$\,dB object-PSNR target, with means of $23$ vs.\ $145$ steps ($84\%$ fewer). (b)~Quality reached at a PSNR plateau, where the warm-started fit converges about $5.9$\,dB higher, because a cold start must re-traverse the full inter-frame motion each frame and tends to stall early}{figure.caption.41}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.4}Velocity Prediction and Convergence}{55}{subsection.3.3.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-velocity}{{3.3.4}{55}{Velocity Prediction and Convergence}{subsection.3.3.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.5}Frustum Clamping}{55}{subsection.3.3.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-snap}{{3.3.5}{55}{Frustum Clamping}{subsection.3.3.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Frustum clamping over the full 150-frame sequence (means over frames; extent at the last frame). PSNR is ceiling-limited and nearly blind to the failures. The extent exposes the runaway, the leak exposes the parked strays that gating accumulates near the subject, and SSIM exposes the structural cost of losing primitives.}}{55}{table.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:snap}{{3.1}{55}{Frustum clamping over the full 150-frame sequence (means over frames; extent at the last frame). PSNR is ceiling-limited and nearly blind to the failures. The extent exposes the runaway, the leak exposes the parked strays that gating accumulates near the subject, and SSIM exposes the structural cost of losing primitives}{table.caption.42}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.23}{\ignorespaces Per-frame behaviour of the three clamping variants over 150 frames. (a)~Object SSIM, where snapping holds the highest structural quality throughout. (b)~Cloud extent, where without any mechanism the furthest primitive coasts away linearly, the runaway signature. Both mechanisms bound it. (c)~Background leak (log scale), where gating is worst, its parked strays accumulating as a haze near the subject, while snapping reclaims them.}}{56}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:snap-curves}{{3.23}{56}{Per-frame behaviour of the three clamping variants over 150 frames. (a)~Object SSIM, where snapping holds the highest structural quality throughout. (b)~Cloud extent, where without any mechanism the furthest primitive coasts away linearly, the runaway signature. Both mechanisms bound it. (c)~Background leak (log scale), where gating is worst, its parked strays accumulating as a haze near the subject, while snapping reclaims them}{figure.caption.43}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.3.5.1}Distance-Field Clamping and Its Interaction with the Masks}{57}{subsubsection.3.3.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-snapmargin}{{3.3.5.1}{57}{Distance-Field Clamping and Its Interaction with the Masks}{subsubsection.3.3.5.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.24}{\ignorespaces The distance-field margin and the segmentation quality interact. (a)~Primitives flagged by the strict projective test per frame. With noisy masks (margin only) the test nominates ${\sim }75\%$ of the cloud. Hysteresis masks without the margin still oscillate in a snap-lag-snap feedback. The combination settles near a fifth of the cloud. (b)~Teleports actually applied (log scale), where the margin dismisses the borderline majority, leaving an order of magnitude fewer interventions.}}{57}{figure.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:snap-margin-results}{{3.24}{57}{The distance-field margin and the segmentation quality interact. (a)~Primitives flagged by the strict projective test per frame. With noisy masks (margin only) the test nominates ${\sim }75\%$ of the cloud. Hysteresis masks without the margin still oscillate in a snap-lag-snap feedback. The combination settles near a fifth of the cloud. (b)~Teleports actually applied (log scale), where the margin dismisses the borderline majority, leaving an order of magnitude fewer interventions}{figure.caption.44}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.25}{\ignorespaces Background drift over 150 frames. (a)~Leaked opacity (log scale), where under the naive loss the background fills monotonically to near-total opacity. Frozen opacity strands strays an order of magnitude above the default, whose leak stays flat at the $10^{-3}$ level. (b)~Core coverage is blind to the failure, the naive arm covers the core trivially by painting everything.}}{58}{figure.caption.46}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:masking-curves}{{3.25}{58}{Background drift over 150 frames. (a)~Leaked opacity (log scale), where under the naive loss the background fills monotonically to near-total opacity. Frozen opacity strands strays an order of magnitude above the default, whose leak stays flat at the $10^{-3}$ level. (b)~Core coverage is blind to the failure, the naive arm covers the core trivially by painting everything}{figure.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.6}Foreground Masking and Silhouette Supervision}{58}{subsection.3.3.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-masking}{{3.3.6}{58}{Foreground Masking and Silhouette Supervision}{subsection.3.3.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Loss-design ablation over the full 150-frame sequence (means over frames). The full-image loss converts the model into an opaque background painting (leak $\to 0.99$). Freezing opacity strands bright primitives off-object and destroys structural quality.}}{58}{table.caption.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:masking}{{3.2}{58}{Loss-design ablation over the full 150-frame sequence (means over frames). The full-image loss converts the model into an opaque background painting (leak $\to 0.99$). Freezing opacity strands bright primitives off-object and destroys structural quality}{table.caption.45}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.7}Frozen Colour}{59}{subsection.3.3.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-colour}{{3.3.7}{59}{Frozen Colour}{subsection.3.3.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.8}Temporal Attribute Smoothness}{59}{subsection.3.3.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-smoothness}{{3.3.8}{59}{Temporal Attribute Smoothness}{subsection.3.3.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.26}{\ignorespaces Colour freezing over 150 frames. (a)~With colours trainable, appearance drifts monotonically away from frame~0. Frozen colours are bit-identical by construction. (b)~The drift buys no quality, since the per-frame object PSNR of the two arms is statistically indistinguishable.}}{60}{figure.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:freecolors}{{3.26}{60}{Colour freezing over 150 frames. (a)~With colours trainable, appearance drifts monotonically away from frame~0. Frozen colours are bit-identical by construction. (b)~The drift buys no quality, since the per-frame object PSNR of the two arms is statistically indistinguishable}{figure.caption.47}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3.9}Full-Sequence Quality and Cost}{60}{subsection.3.3.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:results-full}{{3.3.9}{60}{Full-Sequence Quality and Cost}{subsection.3.3.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.3.9.1}Quality Over the Capture}{60}{subsubsection.3.3.9.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.27}{\ignorespaces The temporal smoothness prior. (a)~Frame-to-frame attribute fluctuation on the full sequence, where the anchored channels drop by up to two orders of magnitude, the exempt positions are untouched. (b)~The pilot sweep, where jitter suppression is already saturated at $\lambda _{\text  {s}} = 0.3$ while the SSIM cost keeps growing, so the smallest weight is chosen. (c)~The compression rate-distortion front of the full run shifts outward, reaching $43.5\times $ at the default knob vs.\ $27.8\times $ for the baseline, at matched-or-better render fidelity.}}{61}{figure.caption.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:smoothness-results}{{3.27}{61}{The temporal smoothness prior. (a)~Frame-to-frame attribute fluctuation on the full sequence, where the anchored channels drop by up to two orders of magnitude, the exempt positions are untouched. (b)~The pilot sweep, where jitter suppression is already saturated at $\lambda _{\text {s}} = 0.3$ while the SSIM cost keeps growing, so the smallest weight is chosen. (c)~The compression rate-distortion front of the full run shifts outward, reaching $43.5\times $ at the default knob vs.\ $27.8\times $ for the baseline, at matched-or-better render fidelity}{figure.caption.48}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.3.9.2}Trajectories and the Emergent Motion Structure}{61}{subsubsection.3.3.9.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.28}{\ignorespaces The full-resolution 150-frame reconstruction. (a)~Per-frame object PSNR against the ground truth, with the segmented-input reference (dashed), where the reconstruction rides above the fidelity of its own inputs on $149$ of $150$ frames, and the common shape of both curves is the illumination gap, not tracking error. (b)~Object SSIM and core coverage are flat across the capture. Nothing degrades with sequence length.}}{62}{figure.caption.49}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:full-sequence}{{3.28}{62}{The full-resolution 150-frame reconstruction. (a)~Per-frame object PSNR against the ground truth, with the segmented-input reference (dashed), where the reconstruction rides above the fidelity of its own inputs on $149$ of $150$ frames, and the common shape of both curves is the illumination gap, not tracking error. (b)~Object SSIM and core coverage are flat across the capture. Nothing degrades with sequence length}{figure.caption.49}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.3.9.3}The Composited Scene}{62}{subsubsection.3.3.9.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.29}{\ignorespaces Ground truth (top) and reconstruction (bottom) at six instants spanning the 150-frame capture, from camera \texttt  {static1}. The same fixed set of primitives, frozen in count and colour, tracks the walk end to end. The residual speckle at $t = 0$ is inherited from the remaining silhouette holes where the subject camouflages against the rug (Section~\ref {sec:results-bgsub}) and closes as the subject moves onto the neutral floor.}}{63}{figure.caption.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qual-temporal}{{3.29}{63}{Ground truth (top) and reconstruction (bottom) at six instants spanning the 150-frame capture, from camera \texttt {static1}. The same fixed set of primitives, frozen in count and colour, tracks the walk end to end. The residual speckle at $t = 0$ is inherited from the remaining silhouette holes where the subject camouflages against the rug (Section~\ref {sec:results-bgsub}) and closes as the subject moves onto the neutral floor}{figure.caption.50}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {3.3.9.4}Cost}{63}{subsubsection.3.3.9.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.30}{\ignorespaces The reconstruction as a field of trajectories. (a)~Paths of 400 randomly sampled primitives through the capture (colour = time). The body's vertical structure is preserved along the walk, so a primitive keeps denoting the same physical feature. (b)~Cumulative displacement across all $11\,561$ primitives, forming a tight, unimodal distribution, the signature of a cloud that moves as one coherent body.}}{64}{figure.caption.51}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trajectories}{{3.30}{64}{The reconstruction as a field of trajectories. (a)~Paths of 400 randomly sampled primitives through the capture (colour = time). The body's vertical structure is preserved along the walk, so a primitive keeps denoting the same physical feature. (b)~Cumulative displacement across all $11\,561$ primitives, forming a tight, unimodal distribution, the signature of a cloud that moves as one coherent body}{figure.caption.51}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.31}{\ignorespaces The recombined scene. Top, the raw capture from camera \texttt  {static2} (never segmented, shown for reference). Bottom, the merge renderer's composite of the static background reconstruction (a novel view for it) with the per-frame dynamic foreground at three instants.}}{64}{figure.caption.52}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qual-merge}{{3.31}{64}{The recombined scene. Top, the raw capture from camera \texttt {static2} (never segmented, shown for reference). Bottom, the merge renderer's composite of the static background reconstruction (a novel view for it) with the per-frame dynamic foreground at three instants}{figure.caption.52}{}}
